Hours a man lived without lungs thanks to some serious science.

A 33-year-old man with influenza B and an antibiotic-resistant bacterial in=
fection was in such bad shape that his lung tissue was turning into liquid,=
 per (https://arstechnica.com/health/2026/01/custom-machine-kept-man-alive-=
without-lungs-for-48-hours/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9C799tVRAgjMKbz01_pEgVpUarxc5o=
fszFgCpBv2TlWoWml0vrkm0rnN4osA7MpIM_pLozppLl9JfpwzPGf3jJQY2ZMw&_hsmi=3D4017=
14543 ) Ars Technica. With no way for oxygen to reach his blood, everything=
 was shutting down, and he was too ill for a lung transplant.

So, surgeon/researcher Ankit Bharat and his team at Northwestern University=
 did something pretty wild: They took his lungs out =E2=80=94 this is consi=
dered a last resort =E2=80=94 and replaced them with a device they'd engine=
ered to mimic them. This, plus other tactics to keep his heart working, all=
owed the man to live until the sepsis in his body resolved and a lung trans=
plant could be completed.

Though the paper documenting this case is new, the surgery actually occurre=
d two years ago. And, yes, the patient is doing just fine.

