lank">per</a> <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">Ars Technica</span>. With=
 no way for oxygen to reach his blood, everything was shutting down, and he=
 was too ill for a lung transplant. <br><br></p>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">So, surgeon/researcher Ankit Bharat and his t=
eam at Northwestern University did something pretty wild: They took his lun=
gs out =E2=80=94 this is considered a last resort =E2=80=94 and replaced th=
em with a device they'd engineered to mimic them. This, plus other tactics =
to keep his&nbsp;heart working, allowed the man to live until the sepsis in=
 his body resolved and a lung transplant could be completed. <br><br></p>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">Though the paper documenting this case is new=
, the surgery actually occurred two years ago. And, yes, the patient is doi=
ng just fine.&nbsp;<br><br></p></div></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
